The problem involves calculating all the distinct spanning trees of a given graph that are rooted at two specific vertices, i.e., f(x,y). The BFS tree is determined based on a specific BFS algorithm, which ensures that the shortest distance between any two nodes in the tree is equal to the shortest distance in the original graph. This BFS tree needs to span the entire graph, and given the constraints of the problem, we can conclude that it's one distinct BFS tree modulo 998 244 353.

The solution provided at the question asks you to compute f(i,j) for all i, j where f(i,j) is the number of BFS trees that are rooted at the given vertices. Since the problem ensures that the BFS trees are unique (based on BFS algorithm which guarantees shortest path among nodes), f(i,j) can be computed uniquely in O(m) time since the BFS time is linear in terms of the number of nodes and augment or communicate.

But here the actual utility and elegance of the problem is that we don't need to actually compute BFS trees; just computing the paths would suffice because BFS guarantees the shortest distance to and from any node in a graph. BFS gives us the shortest paths when run on any DFS, implementing BFS ensures that shortest distance check and BFS can be run on any starting node.

